    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "7dc33343e5b9949ab6bc3293e71f9ca2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2014,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka launches AgriConnect Joint Implementation Plan to transform agriculture, create jobs\n\nThe government of Sri Lanka, in partnership with the World Bank Group and International Fund for Agricultural Development (IFAD), yesterday announced the launch of the Sri Lanka AgriConnect Joint Implementation Plan, a coordinated effort to transform the country\u2019s agrifood sector, create jobs and strengthen food security.\n\n\u201cSri Lanka is committed to transforming our agrifood sector from one that primarily produces commodities to one that creates greater value, better jobs and stronger opportunities for rural communities. Our farmers, fishers and rural communities are not simply beneficiaries of development; they are partners in building a stronger, more productive and more resilient economy. The AgriConnect Joint Implementation Plan will directly catalyse the government action under our Public Investment Programme, helping us connect the farmers to finance, technology and markets while creating better jobs, attracting private investment and expanding opportunities across rural Sri Lanka,\u201d said Prime Minister of Sri Lanka Dr. Harini Amarasuriya.\n\nAligned with the government\u2019s national development agenda and Public Investment Programme 2026-2030 as well as the World Bank Group\u2019s Country Partnership Framework, the plan brings together the government of Sri Lanka, World Bank Group, IFAD, development partners, financial institutions, agribusinesses and farmers around a shared vision for a more productive, climate-resilient and market-oriented food sector.\n\nThe initiative will help 900,000 farmers gain better access to agricultural value chains by 2030. This will be backed by US $ 550 million in the World Bank Group investments and US $ 115 million from the IFAD and help mobilise up to US $ 170 million in private capital over the next five years.\n\nAgriculture and food processing account for at least 15 percent of Sri Lanka\u2019s GDP, 30 percent of goods exports and one in three jobs."
  },
