    "language": "en",
    "md5": "700fa22ad2072dd98c0e73c6bc188232",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1851,
    "chunk_text": "The initiative will help 900,000 farmers gain better access to agricultural value chains by 2030. This will be backed by US $ 550 million in the World Bank Group investments and US $ 115 million from the IFAD and help mobilise up to US $ 170 million in private capital over the next five years.\n\nAgriculture and food processing account for at least 15 percent of Sri Lanka\u2019s GDP, 30 percent of goods exports and one in three jobs.\n\nAs the first country in South Asia to officially join AgriConnect, Sri Lanka will help build partnerships between farmers and agribusinesses and advance agriculture finance, digital agriculture and agtech, alongside cold chains, connectivity, climate-resilient irrigation, skills and research.\n\nThe initiative will focus on value chains with strong potential for value addition and exports, including coconut, cinnamon, tea, fisheries as well as dairy, poultry, aquaculture and horticulture. It will also support greater economic opportunities in lagging regions, including the Northern and Eastern provinces.\n\nAgriConnect will also strengthen coordination among the government, development partners and private sector to tackle the policy barriers, address financing constraints and unlock opportunities at scale.\n\n\u201cThere is a real opportunity to build on the progress already made in Sri Lanka\u2019s rural economy and ensure that investments continue to reach smallholder farmers. Through AgriConnect, we can maintain this momentum and bring together the scale, financing and expertise of all partners to invest in the first mile of food systems. This means translating our shared vision into tangible realities for rural transformation: driving capital into rural areas, generating jobs and building resilience against future shocks,\u201d said IFAD Country Operations Department Associate Vice-President Donal Brown."
  },
