    "chunk_text": "On an annual basis, food contributed 2.87 percentage points to headline inflation. Sea fish was the largest individual upward contributor within the food basket, adding 0.74 percentage points, followed by vegetables at 0.52 percentage points, fresh fruits at 0.43 percentage points and dried fish at 0.37 percentage points.\n\nThe lower coconut and rice prices cushioned the rise, subtracting 0.31 and 0.22 percentage points, respectively. Limes, despite becoming more expensive during October, remained cheaper than a year earlier and reduced annual inflation by 0.22 percentage points.\n\nThe non-food items contributed 5.42 percentage points to annual inflation, with the fuel-related expenditure prominent among the drivers.\n\nPetrol and other fuel within the transport category accounted for 1.42 percentage points of the annual increase in the overall index. The bus fares contributed 0.27 percentage points, motor cars 0.20 percentage points, diesel 0.19 percentage points and vehicle servicing 0.17 percentage points. School transport added another 0.13 percentage points.\n\nWithin housing and utilities, the higher rents contributed 0.52 percentage points, followed by LP gas at 0.28 percentage points, electricity at 0.19 percentage points and maintenance materials at 0.16 percentage points.\n\nRestaurants and hotels added 0.87 percentage points to annual inflation, while education contributed 0.31 percentage points, reflecting the increases in international school fees, higher education course fees, tuition and vocational training charges.\n\nMiscellaneous goods and services contributed 0.21 percentage points, household furnishings and maintenance 0.15 percentage points and health 0.10 percentage points. Recreation and culture, along with alcoholic beverages, tobacco and narcotics, recorded negative annual contributions."
