    "md5": "21e925d7e34d14421a28c0622c9bc5f1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1546,
    "chunk_text": "However, Sri Lankans who have a habit of reading too much into geopolitics fail to account for the primary contributory factor for recurrent deadlocks and abandonment of high-profile investment projects. They are all domestic: Institutional incompetence, regulatory bottlenecks and ad hoc policy changes have seriously dented Sri Lanka\u2019s investment climate.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s institutional structure is more attuned to finding excuses for not doing than contributing to growth. The same paralysis continues even after a project is formally abandoned. One striking example is the Adani Wind energy farm, which withdrew after the government sought to renegotiate the energy purchase contract. Leave aside the political narrative, the government\u2019s decision for reappraisal may have its logic, later vindicated by tenders granted to two local producers at the purchase price of less than US$ 0.05 a unit against Adani\u2019s $ 0.086.\n\nHowever, since then the government has failed to follow up with a Swiss challenge tender or call new tenders for the abandoned Adani project. The bureaucracy in this country is more apt at procrastination than initiative. The opportunity cost is not just the delayed cheaper renewable energy. In a year or two, the project would be unviable due to local protests, often instigated by vested interests.\n\nWhile the Port Authority plans for a PPP in place of the abandoned joint venture for a logistical hub, the authorities should make sure the project would not be a repetition of the recurrent institutional maladies."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
